  "id": "COMPOUND:30842 - PROTEIN:6138",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex network of interactions where a single compound, (2S,3S,4S,5R,6R)-6-[[(3S,4S,6aR,8aR,9S,10R,14bR)-9-[(2S,3R,4S,5R)-3,5-dihydroxy-4-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-10-hydroxy-4-(hydroxymethyl)-4,6a,6b,8a,11,11,14b-heptamethyl-1,2,3,4a,5,6,7,8,9,10,12,12a,14,14a-tetradecahydropicen-3-yl]oxy]-5-[(2S,3R,4S,5R,6R)-4,5-dihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-3,4-dihydroxyoxane-2-carboxylic acid, acts as a target for multiple enzymes and pathways. This compound is linked to Caspase-5, which in turn is a target of several other molecules, including WS-5995 B, Hex-3-en-1-yl 2-methylbut-2-enoate, and 2,4-Bis[(4-hydroxyphenyl)methyl]-5-methoxy-3-[2-(3-methoxyphenyl)ethyl]phenol. All of these molecules ultimately target the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, indicating a potential role in the regulation of proteasome activity. The evidence suggests that the compound may influence proteasome function through the activation of Caspase-5, which is a key regulator of proteasome-dependent processes. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which the compound could modulate proteasome activity, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
